---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { parse as parseYaml } from 'yaml';
import AboutTabs from '../../components/AboutTabs.astro';
import Shell from '../../components/Shell.astro';
import { aboutCounts, aiOnlyLine, consideredLine, pickCountPhrase, shortSourceName, themeCountPhrase } from '../../lib/about-stats.mjs';
import { formatRunStamp, latestWeek, synthesisExcerpt } from '../../lib/weeks';

const week = latestWeek();
if (!week) {
  throw new Error('About needs a published week');
}

const sourceFile = parseYaml(readFileSync(join(process.cwd(), 'config', 'sources.yaml'), 'utf8'));
const enabled = (sourceFile?.sources ?? []).filter((source) => source && source.enabled === true);
const aiOnlyNames = enabled
  .filter((source) => source.ai_only === true)
  .map((source) => shortSourceName(source.display_name));
const counts = aboutCounts(week);
const sourcesTotal = enabled.length;
const runLine = consideredLine(counts);
const themesPhrase = themeCountPhrase(counts.themes);
const picksPhrase = pickCountPhrase(counts.picks);
const aiOnlySentence = aiOnlyLine(aiOnlyNames);
const ranAt = formatRunStamp(week.generated_at);
---

<Shell
  title="About: how it works · Dan's AI Digest"
  description={synthesisExcerpt(week.briefing.synthesis)}
  canonical="/about/how-it-works"
  week={week}
  active="about"
  scope="latest"
>
  <AboutTabs tab="how-it-works" />
  <div class="g hph">
    <p
      class="run"
      data-considered={counts.considered}
      data-stories={counts.stories}
      data-merged={counts.merged}
      data-also-seen={counts.alsoSeen}
      data-left-out={counts.leftOut}
      data-themes={counts.themes}
      data-picks={counts.picks}
      data-sources={sourcesTotal}
      data-ai-only={aiOnlyNames.length}
    >
      {runLine}
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g hbody">
    <section class="steps-col" aria-label="How a week is built">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step" id="sources">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Who picks the sources</h3>
            <p>
              Dan picks every source, <a href="/about/sources">{sourcesTotal} right now</a>: newsletters, blogs and
              YouTube channels. Nothing outside that list gets in. Most weeks all {sourcesTotal} are read; a source that
              blocks automated reading is skipped that week.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="filters">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>What is filtered out before review</h3>
            <p>These never reach the AI's review and are not in the count above:</p>
            <ul class="rl">
              <li>Anything published outside the Sunday to Saturday week.</li>
              <li>Duplicates: the same link, or near-identical titles.</li>
              <li>{aiOnlySentence}</li>
              <li>Old pages re-dated into the week.</li>
              <li>AI-written roundups, such as Latent Space's AINews.</li>
              <li>Subscriber-only posts.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="rules">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>The inclusion rules</h3>
            <p>The AI reads each remaining item. It gets in if it meets at least one rule:</p>
            <ul class="rl">
              <li><b>New.</b> A launch, a finding, a policy move, or real data.</li>
              <li><b>Argued.</b> A real argument or analysis, not just a pointer to someone else's.</li>
              <li><b>Relevant.</b> It touches a theme from this week or a running story.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Left out: shorts with no substance, quotes with no commentary, and minor version releases. Every item left
              out keeps a written reason, so the call can be checked. An item that only mentions AI in passing is left
              out here too. There is no cap on how many items one source can contribute.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="merge">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>How duplicate stories are merged</h3>
            <p>
              When several sources cover the same story, they become one entry and the others are listed as also covered.
              The lead source is picked this way: a full article beats a short link, a piece about the story beats a
              roundup, and after that the source that states the key facts wins. Items with no article text, such as a
              bare video description, become short links under Also seen. An item left out is never added back by merging.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section class="side-col" aria-label="How a week is built, continued">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step" id="summaries">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
          <div>
            <h3>How summaries are written</h3>
            <p>
              Each summary is 60 words or fewer, plus a one-sentence takeaway, written only from that article's text or
              the video's transcript. Nothing is added from elsewhere. If a summary can't be made, the story still appears
              with the note: The summary couldn't be generated this week.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="themes">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
          <div>
            <h3>How the lead, themes, Top {counts.picks} and Number of the week are chosen</h3>
            <p>These are the AI's judgment, guided by stated criteria, with no weighted score.</p>
            <ul class="rl">
              <li>
                <b>Lead and themes.</b> Written together: a lead of one to three sentences, then {themesPhrase}, each a
                short label and one sentence of 25 to 35 words. A story can sit in two themes.
              </li>
              <li id="top5">
                <b>Top {counts.picks}.</b> {picksPhrase} the AI judges most worth reading. There is no rank order.
              </li>
              <li>
                <b>Number of the week.</b> The figure from the week that passes the most of five tests: it changes the
                picture, it comes firsthand, it has a comparison, it is about something real, and it is new this week but
                will still matter in a month.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="wrong">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">7</span>
          <div>
            <h3>What it can get wrong</h3>
            <ul class="rl">
              <li>It can misjudge what counts as new or argued and leave out a good piece. The written reasons make that checkable.</li>
              <li>Merging can join two different stories, or miss that two writeups are the same one.</li>
              <li>Short summaries drop nuance. Check the source before quoting a figure.</li>
              <li>The themes, Top {counts.picks} and Number of the week are judgment calls, not measurements.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="when">
          <span class="sn" aria-hidden="true">8</span>
          <div>
            <h3>When it runs</h3>
            <p>Runs Sundays at 7:29 AM ET. This week ran {ranAt}.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
  </div>
</Shell>
